Here are a few ways to parse `--port` and `--verbose` flags in Node.js:

## Built-in `parseArgs()` (Node.js 16.17.0+)

```javascript
const { parseArgs } = require('util');

const { values, positionals } = parseArgs({
  options: {
    port: {
      type: 'string',
      short: 'p',
    },
    verbose: {
      type: 'boolean',
      short: 'v',
    },
  },
});

console.log('Port:', values.port || 3000);
console.log('Verbose:', values.verbose || false);
```

## Manual parsing with `process.argv`

```javascript
const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const flags = {};

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === '--port' || args[i] === '-p') {
    flags.port = args[i + 1];
    i++;
  } else if (args[i] === '--verbose' || args[i] === '-v') {
    flags.verbose = true;
  }
}

console.log('Port:', flags.port || 3000);
console.log('Verbose:', flags.verbose || false);
```

## Usage

```bash
node script.js --port 8080 --verbose
node script.js -p 5000 -v
node script.js --verbose
```

The built-in `parseArgs()` is cleaner and recommended for modern Node.js. The manual approach works with any version but requires more code.
